  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "Three Latin roots describe the body’s basic needs. <b>viv</b> (also <b>vit</b>) means “live” or “life”: a <i>vivid</i> color is full of life, and to <i>survive</i> is to live on. <b>vor</b> means “eat”: a <i>carnivore</i> eats meat and an <i>herbivore</i> eats plants. <b>somn</b> means “sleep”: <i>insomnia</i> is not being able to sleep."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-scene",
        "opts": {
          "scene": {
            "bg": "classroom",
            "cast": [
              {
                "who": "edwin",
                "x": 26,
                "pose": "pointup",
                "mood": "happy",
                "face": "right",
                "say": "…and then…"
              },
              {
                "who": "dev",
                "x": 68,
                "pose": "sit",
                "mood": "sleepy",
                "face": "left",
                "seat": "desk"
              }
            ],
            "fx": [
              {
                "kind": "zzz",
                "on": "c1",
                "at": "head"
              }
            ],
            "labels": [
              {
                "text": "SOMN = SLEEP",
                "x": 50,
                "y": 92
              }
            ],
            "alt": "A student nods off at his desk while a professor keeps talking."
          }
        },
        "cap": "A student nods off at his desk while a professor keeps talking."
      },
      {
        "t": "key",
        "html": "<b>viv</b>, <b>vit</b> = <b>life, live</b>. <b>vor</b> = <b>eat</b>. <b>somn</b> = <b>sleep</b> (and a related word, <b>sopor</b>, means deep sleep)."
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "These roots often describe people, not just bodies. A <b>convivial</b> host loves living it up together with friends. A <b>voracious</b> reader devours books. A <b>somnolent</b> afternoon makes everyone drowsy, and a <b>soporific</b> speech puts listeners to sleep."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "The parts and their words"
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-roots",
        "opts": {
          "max": 10,
          "roots": [
            "viv",
            "vor",
            "somn"
          ]
        },
        "cap": "Words on the list built on viv, vor and somn."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "Words taken apart"
      },
      {
        "t": "table",
        "head": [
          "Word and meaning",
          "Parts, and what they say"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "<b>revive</b><br>to bring back to life or use",
            "re- <i>again</i> + <b>viv</b> <i>live</i> + -e<br>→ “live again”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>convivial</b><br>friendly and lively; fond of good company",
            "con- <i>together</i> + <b>viv</b> <i>live</i> + -ial<br>→ “living (feasting) together”"
          ],
          [
            "<b><i>vivid</i></b><br>bright and lively",
            "<b>viv</b> <i>life</i> + -id<br>→ “full of life”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>voracious</b><br>wanting great amounts of food or of an activity",
            "<b>vor</b> <i>eat</i> + -acious<br>→ “eating greedily”"
          ],
          [
            "<b><i>carnivore</i></b><br>an animal that eats meat",
            "carn <i>meat</i> + <b>vor</b> <i>eat</i> + -e<br>→ “meat-eater”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>somnolent</b><br>sleepy; drowsy",
            "<b>somn</b> <i>sleep</i> + -olent<br>→ “sleepy”"
          ],
          [
            "<b><i>insomnia</i></b><br>trouble falling or staying asleep",
            "in- <i>without</i> + <b>somn</b> <i>sleep</i> + -ia<br>→ “without sleep”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>soporific</b><br>tending to cause sleep",
            "<b>sopor</b> <i>deep sleep</i> + -ific <i>making</i><br>→ “sleep-making”"
          ]
        ],
        "cap": "Each word, what it means now, and its parts. Words in italics are familiar words that are not on the list."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "title": "Use the parts",
        "q": "The warm room and the long, slow lecture were <b>soporific</b>; half the class nodded off. What does the word mean?",
        "steps": [
          "Take it apart: <b>sopor</b> (deep sleep) + <b>-ific</b> (making, as in <i>horrific</i>, which makes horror).",
          "Literally: “sleep-making.”",
          "The sentence fits: half the class nodded off."
        ],
        "a": "Tending to cause sleep."
      },
